      "findings": [
        {
          "check": "clinical_scope_and_decisive_support",
          "result": "clear",
          "finding": "Abrupt pain, pallor, coolness, absent pedal arterial signals and toe sensory loss with preserved movement and venous signal support a salvageable, marginally threatened acute limb ischemia presentation. Immediate UFH and emergency vascular-team involvement are directly supported by the retained 2019 review, with DUS/CTA for a viable or marginally threatened limb. The recent meta-analysis separately supports prompt, individually selected revascularization. Candidate A wrongly defers both anticoagulation and vascular-team activation while imaging is obtained.",
          "source_ids": [
            "31416204",
            "42097877"
          ]
        },
        {
          "check": "population_safety_and_evidence_limits",
          "result": "clear",
          "finding": "This adult has no active or recent major bleeding, recent intracranial event or known HIT. The sound answer contains no exact UFH dose or universally preferred revascularization technique. The 2019 source is a secondary clinical review, not a primary guideline; it meets the unchanged calendar-year gate but is older than seven rolling years. Its retained blanket open-revascularization statement for nonviable limbs is not adopted. The authored entry explicitly limits the revascularization rationale to a salvageable limb, and the newer source supports patient-tailored modality selection. Imaging must be urgent and support prompt treatment, as the answer states.",
          "source_ids": [
            "31416204",
            "42097877"
          ]
        },
        {
          "check": "literal_passage_entailment",
          "result": "clear",
          "finding": "Both reviewers select passage p12 for immediate UFH and continuous vascular-team access and p14 for DUS/CTA in stages I/IIa. The recent meta-analysis passages p0 and p3 substantiate urgency and multidisciplinary tailoring. The extra p8 selected by one reviewer reinforces viable-limb imaging. Each resolved quote equals its generated source handle; none relies only on a guideline title or scope abstract.",
          "source_ids": [
            "31416204",
            "42097877"
          ]
        },
        {
          "check": "public_answer_cues",
          "result": "clear",
          "finding": "The exact public payload reconstructed using the CI-revision code contains objective ischemic findings and contraindication history, without the private answer, hard hook, divergence metadata or source claims. The title, question and clinical note do not prescribe heparin, specialist activation, imaging order or a preferred candidate. Stating absence of prior HIT is clinically relevant safety data, not a completed management recommendation."
        },
        {
          "check": "pair_distinctness",
          "result": "clear",
          "finding": "The bundled examination concerns expedited carotid endarterectomy after recent ipsilateral amaurosis fugax and confirmed severe carotid stenosis in a fit octogenarian. The practice entry concerns immediate anticoagulation and vascular-team activation for acute lower-limb ischemia. The decisions, anatomy, timescale and decisive contraindication assessment differ."
        }
      ],
