      "findings": [
        {
          "check": "clinical_scope_and_decisive_support",
          "result": "clear",
          "finding": "Chronic C5 SCI, reproducible symptomatic hypertension during bladder filling, documented involuntary detrusor contractions and resolution with drainage establish bladder-triggered AD associated with NDO. Reliable caregiver-assisted intermittent catheterization and an ineffective, intolerable oral oxybutynin trial support discussing intradetrusor botulinum toxin with neuro-urology while maintaining reliable emptying. The retained narrative review supports this NDO treatment context and procedural risks; the 2026 systematic review independently supports possible reduction in AD episodes and severity. Lidocaine evidence is small and conflicting, and does not support replacing treatment of the documented bladder-filling trigger.",
          "source_ids": [
            "38393175",
            "42430939",
            "35962043"
          ]
        },
        {
          "check": "population_safety_and_evidence_limits",
          "result": "clear",
          "finding": "The patient is asymptomatic with baseline blood pressure and an emptied bladder at the visit. The assessed question is elective prevention, so the sound answer does not purport to supply an acute hypertensive AD algorithm. It acknowledges retention and procedure-associated AD, preserves catheterization, and promises no universal benefit. The evidence does not prove superiority over every alternative oral treatment or long-term cardiovascular benefit. The narrative source discusses onabotulinumtoxinA/Botox specifically; the entry proposes specialist discussion and does not claim formulation equivalence, prescribe dose substitutions or assert FDA approval for AD reduction. The 2026 review has a future October issue label but documented July 8 electronic publication.",
          "source_ids": [
            "38393175",
            "42430939"
          ]
        },
        {
          "check": "literal_passage_entailment",
          "result": "clear",
          "finding": "The two reviewers select the same substantive passages: the 2026 review p0 for lesion-risk context and p1 for NDO-treatment results; the narrative review p33 for bladder-related triggers, p31 for refractory NDO indication and retention, and p32 for injection-provoked AD; the lidocaine review p1 for small inconsistent studies and inadequate evidence. All 14 resolved quote instances match these exact handles. Scope-only AUA abstract material is not used to establish an omitted acute management algorithm.",
          "source_ids": [
            "38393175",
            "42430939",
            "35962043"
          ]
        },
        {
          "check": "public_answer_cues",
          "result": "clear",
          "finding": "The public title, question, note, urodynamic findings and impression identify the syndrome and current stable state, without naming the preferred future preventive treatment. The private answer, hard hook and decision-scope key are absent. Prior oxybutynin intolerance and reliable catheterization are necessary treatment-selection facts, rather than instructions to choose the sound candidate."
        },
        {
          "check": "pair_distinctness",
          "result": "clear",
          "finding": "The bundled practice concerns early poststroke focal plantarflexor overactivity and distinguishes tone reduction from orthotic mobility assistance. Although both entries discuss botulinum toxin, this examination assesses SCI-associated bladder NDO/AD prevention with retention and procedure-trigger risks. The neurologic disorder, target organ, indication, comparator, intended benefit and safety assessment differ."
        }
      ],
